# `alt` / `title` for images and links

SEO/audit tools report **Title: /** when `title` is missing (they fall back to the page path `/`).

## Images

- Every `<img>` / `next/image` needs a **non-empty** `alt` and matching `title`.
- Amenity / nearby icons: `alt` and `title` from `altTag` → name/title → sensible fallback. Never omit `title`.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD — audits show Title: /
<img src={url} alt={item.altTag || item.title} />

// ✅ GOOD
const label = item.altTag || item.title || "Amenity icon";
<img src={url} alt={label} title={label} />
```

## Links (including `#` section anchors)

- Every `<a>` / `<Link>` that audits check needs a descriptive `title`.
- Section tabs (`#overview`, `#amenities`, `#gallery`, etc.) must set `title` from the visible label — never leave it unset.
- External links (Google Maps, etc.) need an explicit `title` too — audits otherwise report **Title: /**.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD
<a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>
<a href={mapHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View On Map</a>

// ✅ GOOD
<a href={`#${id}`} title={`${label} — ${projectName}`}>{label}</a>
<a
  href={mapHref}
  target="_blank"
  rel="noopener noreferrer"
  title={`View ${projectName} on Google Maps`}
>
  View On Map
</a>
```

## Dynamic titles

- Use template literals. Never put `{variable}` inside a normal string.

```jsx
// ❌ BAD — literal braces in the tooltip
title="Projects in {projectDetail.city}"

// ✅ GOOD
title={`Projects in ${projectDetail.city}`}
```
